# Downloaded source scans and their text layers live in sources/ and are
# deliberately NOT tracked in git:
#   - archive.org PDFs routinely exceed github.com's 100 MiB per-file block
#   - binaries don't delta-compress, so every re-download bloats history forever
#   - LFS bandwidth (10 GiB/mo on Free/Pro) is spent by each fresh clone, and
#     cloud sessions clone fresh
#   - the collection may include in-copyright editions; a repo makes you the
#     redistributor
#
# What IS tracked: the catalog, the research register, the reports, the tools,
# and reports/download_log.jsonl — which records, for every item fetched, its
# archive.org identifier, title, creator, date, rights status, page count,
# file sizes and the opening of its text layer. That is enough to reconstruct
# the collection from scratch, and it diffs properly.
sources/
